all right let's return now uh to the  race to become the next leader of the  conservative party and of course our  next prime minister i'm very pleased to  say that joining me now is the  conservative mp sir ian duncan smith and  former leader of course of the  conservation industry who is backing us  are you the campaign manager well i'm  helping overall oversee the campaign but  uh well essentially there are plenty of  other people doing stuff absolutely  right so look um let's start with the  cost of living crisis yes it's the guys  are not going to get together let's  trust them which you know are not going  to get together at all why is that and  shouldn't they  no i don't think so this is a contest to  be the leader of the conservative party  and then obviously the prime minister  uh one of those people is not in cabinet  the other one is it would be quite wrong  for example of liz because of the  campaign to try and direct what the  government is doing at the moment to  suit herself i don't know what they  would achieve together by agreeing what  i don't reassure people who are really  anxious about their bills going on i  think the government can reassure people  that they're looking at it right now the  government is actually going through  what the options are where the problems  exist and we need to understand that so  there's a plan in place when we get to  the point where we have a prime minister  with options should that need a rise and  that's exactly where we are and i think  that's the important bit the government  has all the mechanisms it has all the  enormous power of government to be able  to look at this to get the facts right  the figures right  and to look at the way they do it i mean  i was critical of the 50 we just had a  15 billion pound payout which is gone  and we're still waiting for half of that  to get through to people you know and  it's still not enough as well well we  say it's not enough but that's because  things are shifting so what we're saying  is this money is already going out at  the moment much has not landed in  people's pockets uh and i'm critical in  the sense that i don't believe that we  should have handed out money across the  board to lots of people like you and me  where we're not in the same predicament  as people on low income so i would like  to see if we have to do this much more  targeted on people on low income  targeted support work as necessary and  that can be done by the government prior  the leadership election being completed  through something like universal credit  which you of course designed yeah i  would like to see you know it's a  personal view i'd like to see universal  credit use more because it's the only  system we have which targets whole  households it knows what the household  income is it knows therefore who really  needs the money so instead of spreading  the money all the way up the chain  to 400 pounds to you and me i think the  reality is that this is what liz trust  has been talking about we want targeted  support if we have to do this we have to  look at the events of what's going on  and see what the forecasts are and then  make that decision and one of the  criticisms and one of the reasons why  people should get around the table now  is that to get those uh increases in  universal credit or whatever change  we're gonna make whether it's a taper  rate or an or an uplift you need to  start that process now and if you don't  start it now you're not gonna make it  for october the first well the key thing  is you have to get this right you have  to get it focused if you try and rush  this process and i've been in government  long enough to know when things happen  and people go bang we're going to have  to sort this out what happens is you  waste a lot of money and you miss the  targets you need to get to that's why  it's really important for government  right now to do it liz trust has been  crystal clear from the beginning that  should the need arise she will do  whatever is necessary but right now on a  campaign is not feasible for two  candidates to literally make up uh  amounts of money on the back of an  envelope what she wants to do on day one  to start with is to make sure that we  reduce the burden of taxation because  why  right now we face the potential of a  recession if you have a recession on top  of this cost of living crisis it will be  an utter human disaster we need to avoid  recession  at all costs in the course of this  winter we need to do something about  that that's where lower tax is important  but getting targeted help to people she  said she's certainly prepared to do and  look at as and when the government and  of course you'll know that there is a  debate where the cutting taxes will  stave off the recession which the bank  of england says is coming but let's  stick with universal of course it will  because let me just what can i just  explain something to you quickly the  bank of england's forecasts now for  recession are based on the highest level  of tax that we've had in a generation if  we start to bring that down yep their  forecasts shift all the independent  economists  show that the best way to stay stable is  to be out of that recession forecast if  inflation increases as well caused by  those taxes they're bringing interest  rates in to get inflation down by all  means do that but don't kill people's  eco economics off and put them on on the  serene you know that there are  economists who can agree on the other  side who will say that well cutting  taxes not many independent economists  disagree with most of them i mean some  of them some of them do but let's stick  with universal credit how long would it  take for that money to go through to  people well that's the key question uh  and that's why the government needs to  look at that right now and the key i  mean immediately because by october the  first year bills are going to go up so  will it be there by then yeah but the  key things with benefit systems is you  have to change things through  legislation quickly the question is how  quickly can you do that to make changes  to this process and those are the key  elements that have got to be looked at  now by the government which is why the  government is right to be looking at it  now in preparation for somebody that  takes over so they have instantaneous  possibilities there that they can pull  the levers and get them you can't  guarantee that the extra money will be  there by the first of october well it  depends what you're actually asking them  to do that's what the government's got  to look at all i'm saying to you is  in in line with the urgency of the  requirement and people's incomes and  those who most need it that's the  important point the reality is the  government has to find ways to make sure  that that money gets to those targeted  individuals that's what the government  needs to look at now which is why as i  said earlier on there's no point in two  candidates meeting together to try and  figure out what they would do they're  not in command of the details the  government is here to do that with the  government so the government will do  anything because the government said  they have to wait until the candidates  no no no the government has been working  on this for weeks by the way i know  because i talk to government ministers  who have been working with their  departments to do it so they've been  working for some time what they're doing  now is getting ready to make sure those  decisions are able to be made should  they need to be made they will make  those decisions on the basis of solid  advice argument well maybe i'll put it  to you that the assurance is not getting  there for people but i do want to move  on well because you know at the end of  it all they're worried and we have to  make sure that they know that the  government will step in where necessary  i want to  bring something to your attention this  idea of conversion therapy is a policy  that was put in place uh put pushed  through by liz trust when she was a  qualities minister yep this is what you  had to say about conversion therapy just  recently uh this is liz truster's uh  commitment to it yes i agree with that  by the way you you do because you said  you said recently that no i stopped  banning things  you're looking to report on something i  said i said my personal opinion is that  the bill itself  to deliver that isn't in the right shape  to deliver it we have to get the balance  right between what you do to try and  sort people quite rightly to protect  people who make decisions but at the  same time to make sure that you don't  collide with the rights that people have  to freedom of speech and things and  we've got to work that out so this bill  needs to be reworked looked at again  that's my personal view i agree with her  that the point of what we're trying to  do is to get to a point where you can  protect those who need that protection  that's important so this trust will  carry on with the bill to ban gateway  right now it's suspended and we need to  look at that bill as to how we get that  done the key thing that she will carry  on with i mean well liz trust has made  it very clear in that she's in favor of  it quite rightly but my point is as i  was saying the bill itself we need to  make sure delivers what we're after  without the unintended consequences and  knock-ons that go on and that's  important with legislation you can't  rush it of course make sure it's done so  the objective  i completely agree with okay let me talk  to you quickly about uh the climate  crisis you'll know that many  conservative supporters want us to  change our climate targets for 2050. do  you think that because of the situation  right now we should push those targets  back  well i think what we've committed to is  reaching those targets of 2050 they're  very advanced there's a lot that needs  to be done clearly but the key point is  we have to do it in a way that doesn't  damage lives at the same time so this is  why liz trust has made it very clear  that what she'll do is suspend that levy  that green levy on individuals  costs about 153 pounds for people to do  that at a time when they are really  short of money and they need help that's  one thing that you and fragan have to  deal with and we suspend that for the  moment whilst we make sure we tackle the  cost of living crisis we will return  back to the process of making sure we  achieve that 2050 target that's what she  is committed to and that's what she  wants to do but what she wants to make  sure is you don't do it on the backs of  people who can't afford what you're  actually making them pay and that means  their lives will change syrian duncan  smith formerly the conservative back of  this trust appreciate your time this  morning sir

